[Datatype](https://franktisellano.github.io/datatype/), by Frank Tisellano, is a variable font that renders inline bar charts, sparklines, and pie charts straight out of OpenType ligatures. Write `{b:30,70,50,90}` in any element styled with the font and you get a bar chart; no JavaScript, no SVG, no charting library involved. It is, on its own, a delightful piece of typography. But what made me actually want to write about it is that it inverts an idea I sketched out years ago, and I keep finding that the inversion is the interesting part.

> **tl;dr**
> - Datatype turns text like `{b:30,70,50,90}` into inline charts via OpenType ligatures: bar, sparkline, and pie, with weight and width axes.
> - It rhymes with a 2018 piece of mine, [What if: A web font for data](/posts/20180319-data-font-for-life-sciences.html), but inverts it: my version was a font for *reading* dense data, this is a font that *is* the data.
> - The upstream project ships zero accessibility guidance; for any serious use, pair each chart with `aria-hidden` on the glyph span and a visually-hidden data table. There's a copyable pattern below.

## Two opposite definitions of a data font

Back in 2018, at EMBL-EBI, I wrote a long piece called [What if: A web font for data](/posts/20180319-data-font-for-life-sciences.html). My problem was that life-sciences researchers stare at strings like `ENSMUST00000032717` all day, and the tools they were using rendered those strings in fonts that had not been designed with that kind of work in mind. Sequential zeros were ambiguous (is `20000001` six zeros or seven?). Lowercase `l`, uppercase `I`, and numeric `1` could be hard to tell apart in body widths. `BuMpy caPitiLiSation` patterns were brittle to read at a glance. My sketch was a font where the ligature feature did the same kind of work [Fira Code](https://github.com/tonsky/FiraCode) does for code, but pointed at scientific identifiers: chunking long zero runs, disambiguating glyphs, giving the *reader's eye* better handles.

Datatype is also a data font, and it's also built on the OpenType ligature feature. But it inverts every part of the goal. The text isn't there to be read in the normal sense; it's there to be drawn. The ligature substitution doesn't help you decode the characters — it replaces them entirely with a chart. The font isn't a better tool for the reader. The font *is* the data, and the author is the one encoding it.

My old framing asked how a font could help us *see through* data more clearly. Datatype asks how a font can simply *be* the data. Same machinery, opposite direction, and a nicer surprise than I would have predicted in 2018.

There's a third coincidence I couldn't resist noting. Datatype's [OFL license](https://github.com/franktisellano/datatype/blob/main/OFL.txt) credits IBM Plex Mono as the source of its underlying glyphs, and IBM Plex was the typeface I [wrote about back in 2017](/posts/20171112-ibm-plex-font-and-fira.html) when we were evaluating it for EMBL-EBI's scientific data work. The same letterforms that anchored my early thinking about how a typeface should serve data show up, nine years later, as the substrate of a font that *is* the data.

## How you use it

The interface really is just text. Load the font, apply it to an element, write the chart syntax as content:

{% endmarkdown %}

{% codeAndDemo 'html' %}
<style>
  /* Grab the font from https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Datatype, then: */
  .chart {
    font-family: 'Datatype', sans-serif;
    font-feature-settings: 'liga' 1, 'calt' 1;
    font-size: 1.4em;
  }
</style>

<p>Quarterly sales: <span class="chart">{b:40,75,60,90}</span></p>
<p>Pageviews this week: <span class="chart">{l:30,45,40,60,55,80,70}</span></p>
<p>Disk used: <span class="chart">{p:62}</span></p>
{% endcodeAndDemo %}

{% markdown %}

Three chart kinds: `{b:…}` for bars, `{l:…}` for sparklines, `{p:…}` for a single pie. Bars and sparklines accept up to 20 comma-separated values, each in the 0–100 range. The variable axes (`wdth` and `wght`) let you adjust density and visual weight without swapping files.

