## The accessibility gap

For a font that's explicitly built to encode information, the most striking gap in the project is its silence on accessibility. The README, the integration guide, and the specimen site say nothing about screen readers, ARIA, or text alternatives. The GitHub issue tracker has no open or closed threads about it either. Left alone, a string like `{b:18,21,12,12,15,0,0,0,100,94}` gets read aloud verbatim, brace by digit by comma. That's worse than no alternative at all.

The fix isn't conceptually new; it's the [WAI pattern for complex graphics](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/complex/). Mark the glyph span `aria-hidden`, and pair each chart with a visually-hidden data table that assistive technology can navigate as proper rows and cells:

{% endmarkdown %}

{% codeAndDemo 'html' %}
<style>
  .sr-only {
    border: 0; clip-path: inset(50%); height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden; padding: 0; position: absolute;
    white-space: nowrap; width: 1px;
  }
</style>

<p>
  Posts per year, 2017–2020:
  <span class="chart" aria-hidden="true">{b:60,70,40,40}</span>
</p>
<table class="sr-only">
  <caption>Posts per year, 2017–2020</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Posts</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>2017</td><td>6</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2018</td><td>7</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2019</td><td>4</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2020</td><td>4</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
{% endcodeAndDemo %}

{% markdown %}

This works, but it's entirely on the consumer to remember every time. Anyone reaching for Datatype in real work should wrap that pairing in a component or helper (something like `<DataChart kind="bar" values={…} labels={…} />`) so the table is never missed and the markup pattern is single-sourced. The font itself can't enforce it, and nothing in the upstream project will warn you.

## Where it works, where it doesn't

The accessibility gap is the biggest concern, but it's not the only failure surface worth knowing about before reaching for this:

- **Copy-paste**: editors that normalize curly braces or strip ligatures will silently break the syntax. The text looks fine; the chart just doesn't render.
- **Font-load failure**: if the font hasn't loaded yet, or fails to, the raw `{b:…}` text shows through. `font-display: swap` mitigates the flash, but the failure mode is text, not nothing.
- **Fixed value range**: every series has to be normalised into the 0–100 window. You're doing the math; the font is doing the drawing.
- **Honestly, SVG is probably simpler**: for most charts I'd actually reach for in production, a small SVG snippet or a tiny helper would be more robust and more flexible than a font with a custom syntax.

That last one is the one I keep landing on. Datatype is genuinely cool, and the variable-font axes give it more expressiveness than a static SVG sprite, but it isn't categorically lighter or easier than the established alternatives.

## Why I still want an excuse to use it

So why am I still circling it? Partly because it [rhymes with Miriam Suzanne's argument about combining CSS units](/posts/20250927-the-best-css-unit-might-be-a-combination/): the platform usually has a primitive already, and the trick is recognising it. Variable fonts are quietly turning into a fairly expressive runtime, and Datatype is one of the more playful demonstrations of that.

And partly because it's a nice thing to look at an old idea of your own and see someone else carry the same name down the opposite road, and do it well. If you find a real use for it, [let me know](mailto:khawkins98@gmail.com). I'd like to see where it lands.

